WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.340 --> 00:00:07.630
Welcome to the NCBI minute. You should be
seeing a slide that shows introductory stuff

00:00:07.630 --> 00:00:13.640
about the webinar. Today we'll be talking
about SmartBLAST, a new rapid protein application

00:00:13.640 --> 00:00:20.830
tool. With me in the room is Tao Tao, also
from blast-help. He will manage things and

00:00:20.830 --> 00:00:28.560
answer questions. Tom Madden the lead BLAST
developer will join us at the end. If you

00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:33.840
have any questions please be sure to enter
them in the question pod. We are recording

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:40.090
this webinar. It will be available on out
YouTube channel and the Webinars playlist.

00:00:40.090 --> 00:00:46.489
If there are any answers, questions that are
substantive that everybody would be interested

00:00:46.489 --> 00:00:52.750
in, we will have those available on the Materials
section that will be on the ftp site and webinars

00:00:52.750 --> 00:00:58.469
page.
The slides for this particular talk are up

00:00:58.469 --> 00:01:04.280
on the NCBI site. You can see that compressed
URL listed on the slide, http://1.usa.gov/1EvVxN3.

00:01:04.280 --> 00:01:09.759
So the tool I will introduce today is SmartBLAST.
It is designed to rapidly provide a name to

00:01:09.759 --> 00:01:16.310
an unknown protein as well as provide overall
taxonomic context and sequence length context

00:01:16.310 --> 00:01:20.259
for the close matches to the most similar
proteins in the database, and we will talk

00:01:20.259 --> 00:01:25.380
about that in a minute.
One thing I want to mention about SmartBLAST,

00:01:25.380 --> 00:01:31.039
it's an experimental service. We have a new
program at NCBI called PubMed Labs. We are

00:01:31.039 --> 00:01:35.209
putting some services out there early in their
development. We want to get feedback from

00:01:35.209 --> 00:01:40.439
people about how useful they are or how they
would like to see them different than they

00:01:40.439 --> 00:01:45.319
are right now. So I would think of it as a
preliminary tool. Tom Madden has just arrived

00:01:45.319 --> 00:01:48.669
so if you have questions for Tom, he is here.

00:01:48.669 --> 00:01:53.619
Before we dive into SmartBLAST I want to set
the motivation for it. One of the questions

00:01:53.619 --> 00:01:58.969
David Lipman asked, he is our director, is
what are people doing with BLAST? If you look

00:01:58.969 --> 00:02:04.979
at what's going on with the protein BLAST
service, we have about 50,000 blastp searches

00:02:04.979 --> 00:02:06.279
per day.

00:02:06.279 --> 00:02:13.120
If we look at what people are searching with,
the queries have very many similar sequences

00:02:13.120 --> 00:02:19.750
in the database; 40% have exact matches in
the database. If you look at what people are

00:02:19.750 --> 00:02:25.099
doing just based on where, on the BLAST results
pages, most of them are looking at only the

00:02:25.099 --> 00:02:32.480
top 5 matches. That suggests a particular
use case for BLAST. And it's one where BLAST

00:02:32.480 --> 00:02:37.390
is little too much for the job if you think
about it that way. The use case is simply

00:02:37.390 --> 00:02:45.430
to identify or find a name for a query sequence.
Basically a full blastp search is not necessary.

00:02:45.430 --> 00:02:49.689
It's kind of inefficient if that's what you're
trying to do. That's the motivation for the

00:02:49.689 --> 00:02:53.219
SmartBLAST tool that I will talk about now.

00:02:53.219 --> 00:02:58.370
SmartBLAST is particularly good at finding
those close matches and giving you some context

00:02:58.370 --> 00:03:05.859
for them. That is the URL [blast.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/smartblast/]
directly linked on the BLAST homepage. You

00:03:05.859 --> 00:03:08.879
might see the link as an option when you run
a blastp search.

00:03:08.879 --> 00:03:12.959
It has a couple of things going on. One is
the rapid search for close matches. It does

00:03:12.959 --> 00:03:16.749
it in two ways.
It does a blastp search against a small, a

00:03:16.749 --> 00:03:22.180
landmark database of reference species proteomes.
You get two matches back from that. I will

00:03:22.180 --> 00:03:27.489
come back to that. And the other one is a
search against nr, our default protein database,

00:03:27.489 --> 00:03:33.269
but it uses a different algorithm, a k-mer
algorithm, which is an alignment free approach

00:03:33.269 --> 00:03:39.680
that allows you to search nr very quickly.
It generates hits to the most similar sequences,

00:03:39.680 --> 00:03:43.879
and then to make the alignments we use blastp
in blast2sequences to do this.

00:03:43.879 --> 00:03:48.579
It also incorporates with the five matches
you will get back, a multiple alignment that

00:03:48.579 --> 00:03:53.989
incorporates your query sequence and from
there we generate the SmartBLAST graphical

00:03:53.989 --> 00:03:57.769
display. You will see a protein tree that's
going to give you some taxonomic context.

00:03:57.769 --> 00:04:01.769
There is a graphical display that shows the
full length relationships among the query

00:04:01.769 --> 00:04:05.069
and the closest matches in the database.

00:04:05.069 --> 00:04:11.689
This is a slide that I stole from Tom Madden.
Basically, this is the procedure I just talked

00:04:11.689 --> 00:04:17.320
about, you run the fast k-mer search, you
run the blastp search against the 27 proteomes,

00:04:17.320 --> 00:04:20.459
then we use COBALT to generate the SmartBLAST
display.

00:04:20.459 --> 00:04:26.530
One thing to notice on this slide is the difference
in the sizes of the two databases. Nr has

00:04:26.530 --> 00:04:31.620
about 2 times 10 to the 10th residues, while
the reference species proteome database is

00:04:31.620 --> 00:04:35.680
about 100 times smaller and that's why we
can use the BLASTP search there and still

00:04:35.680 --> 00:04:37.750
do it fairly quickly.

00:04:37.750 --> 00:04:43.900
This is a slide that shows, also stolen from
Tom Madden, this nice tree view of organisms

00:04:43.900 --> 00:04:49.139
whose proteomes are in this model organism
database. We've got a pretty broad taxonomic

00:04:49.139 --> 00:04:57.240
spread; it's certainly not comprehensive.
But it does hit many of the most popular organisms

00:04:57.240 --> 00:05:03.280
in our databases, and for all of these we
use annotations for the best assembly for

00:05:03.280 --> 00:05:06.520
that organism's genome that we have.

00:05:06.520 --> 00:05:12.710
This is the heart of the SmartBLAST display.
This is a search that I did with a translation

00:05:12.710 --> 00:05:18.949
of a open reading frame from the lychee nut
transcriptome. I can quickly pick the protein

00:05:18.949 --> 00:05:23.639
sequence that is basically a novel protein
sequence as far as the NCBI database is concerned,

00:05:23.639 --> 00:05:30.610
we don't have that I generated it. I can quickly
find a name for it. It's probably a DNA mismatch

00:05:30.610 --> 00:05:37.470
repair protein. The name I would want to take
from are the green matches, those of a landmark

00:05:37.470 --> 00:05:43.610
genomes. So Arabidopsis would be the one I'd
probably want to grab for the name. This is

00:05:43.610 --> 00:05:48.960
the mlh1 homolog in this plant. We have a
bunch of other plants, and this sets the taxonomic

00:05:48.960 --> 00:05:55.819
context and it gives me the conserved domains.

00:05:55.819 --> 00:05:59.729
And the graphical overview shows me the full
length relationships among those proteins.

00:05:59.729 --> 00:06:07.000
There is the name that we can get from the
reference database, conserved domain results,

00:06:07.000 --> 00:06:12.190
and the multiple alignment used to generate
the graphical views that you see.

00:06:12.190 --> 00:06:18.810
We also have the traditional BLAST report
below it. You can see the three hits from

00:06:18.810 --> 00:06:25.580
nr, and the two hits from the landmark database.
And you also have additional hits that you

00:06:25.580 --> 00:06:29.099
get from the landmark and nr databases.

00:06:29.099 --> 00:06:39.129
Okay so I will do one example. I will go now
to a web browser. There is the BLAST page

00:06:39.129 --> 00:06:43.509
and you see the link to SmartBLAST. If you
follow that materials link, you can do this

00:06:43.509 --> 00:06:48.599
later on your self. There is a text file there
that has some stuff in it. There is more stuff

00:06:48.599 --> 00:06:53.949
here than you are going to do in this webinar.
What I did was the exact same kind of thing

00:06:53.949 --> 00:06:59.699
I did with the lychee nut transcriptome. This
is a sequence from the striped bass transcriptome,

00:06:59.699 --> 00:07:06.349
that I generated by finding an open reading
frame on one of the RNAseq sequences.

00:07:06.349 --> 00:07:12.419
In essence this is an unknown protein. It
is from a vertebrate so that means it's likely

00:07:12.419 --> 00:07:18.110
going to have lots of very close matches in
the NR database. If I really just want to

00:07:18.110 --> 00:07:22.659
have a good name for this protein, I have
a good chance of finding a homolog in, say

00:07:22.659 --> 00:07:27.759
human or zebrafish that would tell me something
about what this protein is.

00:07:27.759 --> 00:07:32.449
I might be able to find some other fish species
that have this homolog in there. I will go

00:07:32.449 --> 00:07:37.139
ahead and do this is as a live search. I will
grab the whole sequence exact except for the

00:07:37.139 --> 00:07:43.930
asterisk that represents a stop codon.
I will copy that and then go to the BLAST

00:07:43.930 --> 00:07:52.500
homepage. I will click on SmartBLAST to get
to it. And I can just put my protein sequence

00:07:52.500 --> 00:07:57.580
in there.
What I want you to see, and one of the beauty

00:07:57.580 --> 00:08:07.449
part of this is how quickly this happens.
This is practically instantaneous.

00:08:07.449 --> 00:08:14.560
And so right away I've got a result. It tells
me pretty much what I need to know. When I

00:08:14.560 --> 00:08:20.919
look at the graphical view, I've basically
got the whole thing. It happens to be a protein

00:08:20.919 --> 00:08:25.069
I would expect to find in any vertebrate genome.
This is the cystic fibrosis transmembrane

00:08:25.069 --> 00:08:29.919
conductance regulator. I can grab the name
from either the zebrafish or the human, the

00:08:29.919 --> 00:08:35.890
protein shown there. It's also cool because
it shows me taxonomic context for the fish

00:08:35.890 --> 00:08:37.670
species I am working with.

00:08:37.670 --> 00:08:42.600
Gratifyingly, this works out just right because
the closest protein in that tree on the left

00:08:42.600 --> 00:08:47.500
side is from a fish in the same family, the
European seabass. And these are fish in a

00:08:47.500 --> 00:08:52.920
different family but all four of them are
in the same group of dishes. The zebrafish

00:08:52.920 --> 00:08:57.850
is in a different group and you can see it
is a more distantly related protein.

00:08:57.850 --> 00:09:03.410
I can generate a full alignment by clicking
here. That opens a new tab.

00:09:03.410 --> 00:09:11.700
There are the five sequences including my
query, which is at the top there. And this

00:09:11.700 --> 00:09:18.230
is the COBALT display that you may or may
not be familiar with. For myself I like to

00:09:18.230 --> 00:09:24.480
see the Expanded view and I like to change
this to Identity. This gives me some... so

00:09:24.480 --> 00:09:29.880
I can see what the differences are. This is
a full-blown multiple sequence alignment.

00:09:29.880 --> 00:09:34.460
So you have a full-length appreciation of
how these proteins are related to each other.

00:09:34.460 --> 00:09:38.910
Which is different than what BLASTP gives
you. You can generate COBALT from a blastp

00:09:38.910 --> 00:09:48.340
search, but this is a handy way to do this
that gives you a nice concise view of everything.

00:09:48.340 --> 00:09:56.510
And then down below I have the five hits that
I get. Two green ones here, which are the

00:09:56.510 --> 00:10:01.470
more are distantly related ones from the landmark
database and then the three here from the

00:10:01.470 --> 00:10:06.640
other fish species that we saw in the tree.
And then these are the additional BLAST hits

00:10:06.640 --> 00:10:15.080
we saved from both the landmark database search
and the NR search.

00:10:15.080 --> 00:10:18.970
And you have the traditional BLAST results
down here if you want to see them.

00:10:18.970 --> 00:10:27.270
The last thing I want to mention before we
close, is that SmartBLAST is part of the PubMed

00:10:27.270 --> 00:10:33.100
Labs feature of NCBI, so it is under rapid
development and we want to hear from you and

00:10:33.100 --> 00:10:36.320
if you will click on this, "Please let us
know what you think", that sends a feedback

00:10:36.320 --> 00:10:41.350
form to the development team, and they can
take your suggestions into considerations

00:10:41.350 --> 00:10:47.540
for making this more useful. I have one slide
left.

00:10:47.540 --> 00:10:54.510
Just to mention PubMed Labs in general, if
you want to know more about this. These are

00:10:54.510 --> 00:10:59.250
experimental services where we are requesting
input early in the development cycle. We have

00:10:59.250 --> 00:11:06.710
a blog that you may know about, called NCBI
Insights. There's a nice article about PubMed

00:11:06.710 --> 00:11:15.280
Labs and also an article there about SmartBLAST.
So I will stop talking now and see if there

00:11:15.280 --> 00:11:20.810
are any questions that have been written in.
I will leave the webinar open for a few minutes

00:11:20.810 --> 00:11:23.860
in case anybody wants to ask questions.

00:11:23.860 --> 00:11:29.390
I see one question in here that I think I
can answer. What if the query sequence is

00:11:29.390 --> 00:11:33.930
much shorter, I guess he means than the database
sequences. What you basically see is that

00:11:33.930 --> 00:11:41.040
in the graphical view, you are still going
to try to align the whole thing, the parts

00:11:41.040 --> 00:11:45.200
of the other proteins that don't align quite
as well as well will be shown. You will have

00:11:45.200 --> 00:11:46.000
a gap.

00:11:46.000 --> 00:11:53.030
Tao says to tell you that insulin works, as
a short query.

00:11:53.030 --> 00:11:58.490
Remember also SmartBLAST is not incredibly
sensitive. If you start really using short

00:11:58.490 --> 00:12:02.970
sequences, it will not give you a lot of results.
I also want to point out that if you want

00:12:02.970 --> 00:12:07.920
to mess around with some of the examples I
have in there, it's not uncommon for you to

00:12:07.920 --> 00:12:13.020
put a sequence in SmartBLAST and get nothing
back. Remember SmartBLAST was designed to

00:12:13.020 --> 00:12:18.310
find very similar sequences. There are plenty
of sequences out there in the universe that

00:12:18.310 --> 00:12:22.940
have nothing similar in the NCBI databases.
For those kinds of sequence, a lot of times

00:12:22.940 --> 00:12:27.880
things from metagenomes, you will have to
do a full-blown BLASTP search.

00:12:27.880 --> 00:12:34.700
So Tom, if anybody has any specific requests
about SmartBLAST they should go ahead and

00:12:34.700 --> 00:12:40.950
use the feedback link. If there's something
you want to request about SmartBLAST, please

00:12:40.950 --> 00:12:43.220
use the feedback link and tells about it .

00:12:43.220 --> 00:12:48.810
So, I have a question, what is the most distant
hit it can find. I don't quite know how to

00:12:48.810 --> 00:12:52.730
answer that.
Tom will take a stab at this one.

00:12:52.730 --> 00:12:59.230
[Tom] As Peter was saying, SmartBLAST is doing
two different kinds of searches. One is the

00:12:59.230 --> 00:13:05.340
kmer search which is in alignment free algorithm
. We find that does pretty well down to 70%

00:13:05.340 --> 00:13:12.480
or so. It will find the close matches. If
you go to run a blastp search you will get

00:13:12.480 --> 00:13:19.420
a lot of stuff that's above 70%. The other
part is that a standard BLASTP search is as

00:13:19.420 --> 00:13:24.410
sensitive as any other blastp search. So,
searching the whole of nr you can go down

00:13:24.410 --> 00:13:29.130
to about 70%; searching the 27 proteomes,
it's kind of a standard blastp.

00:13:29.130 --> 00:13:35.230
[Peter] Right, and for those, you can see
pretty distant things there. It is quite common,

00:13:35.230 --> 00:13:39.490
that I observe that you can find things where
the only matches you get are to the reference

00:13:39.490 --> 00:13:39.820
sequences.

00:13:39.820 --> 00:13:46.630
[Tom] So, yes, that would be possible, and
I guess what Peter calls the landmarks, it

00:13:46.630 --> 00:13:53.160
gives you landmarks of proteomes that might
be fairly distant from your query.

00:13:53.160 --> 00:13:57.690
So this is a question ... it would be nice
if the colored bars showed some representation

00:13:57.690 --> 00:14:05.900
of similarity of the query to the matches.
Again I think that qualifies as a request.

00:14:05.900 --> 00:14:11.050
Write it up and send it to us. We will take
a look at it. That way we have a record and

00:14:11.050 --> 00:14:14.580
we can take a look and see how feasible it
is.

00:14:14.580 --> 00:14:19.610
I think Wayne's question is legitimate. This
is a plant because Wayne is actually one of

00:14:19.610 --> 00:14:24.580
us. He wants to know, do you get in an RID.
And, yes you do get an RID, but currently

00:14:24.580 --> 00:14:29.280
we don't have any way for you to save it.
[Tom] And so basically it is easier to rerun

00:14:29.280 --> 00:14:36.730
it. There is an RID around, but I'd just say
go ahead and rerun it. This is part of PubMed

00:14:36.730 --> 00:14:41.880
Labs and things could change pretty quickly.
We don't want to make any commitment as to

00:14:41.880 --> 00:14:43.690
how to deal with the RID right now.

00:14:43.690 --> 00:14:48.280
[Peter] These searches are so fast and the
only thing you can do is enter one query,

00:14:48.280 --> 00:14:52.320
so there's not a whole lot of advantage to
sending us an RID to ask a question about

00:14:52.320 --> 00:14:58.360
something.
Send us what the query was and we can find

00:14:58.360 --> 00:14:59.160
out what happened.

00:14:59.160 --> 00:15:08.220
And of course the question someone just asked,
is there a command line program, and the answer

00:15:08.220 --> 00:15:11.880
is no, not right now. I don't know if there
are any plans to do that.

00:15:11.880 --> 00:15:16.690
[Tom] No, not right now.
So it would be very complicated to make it

00:15:16.690 --> 00:15:21.450
into a stand alone program.
I think we will go ahead and wrap it up. Thank

00:15:21.450 --> 00:15:27.640
you all for coming. We have several questions
that might be worth compiling. We will go

00:15:27.640 --> 00:15:31.690
ahead and put those in a document. I will
go ahead and stop talking now. Thank you all

00:15:31.690 --> 00:15:32.200
for coming.

